using System.Collections.Generic;

namespace FocusApp;

/// <summary>
/// The "How Focus works" guide in Settings. Plain text, so it's easy to update: add or edit a topic here.
/// </summary>
public static class Guide
{
    public record Topic(string Title, string InspiredBy, string HowItWorks, string Tips);

    public static readonly IReadOnlyList<Topic> Topics = new[]
    {
        new Topic("Home",
            "Ryan's design.",
            "Focus opens on Home: a greeting with today's numbers, today's calendar on the left (opened at the current time, with Show my plan to hide or show your sessions), and your tasks on the right. Today shows what's picked, due, overdue or placed in today's sessions; All shows everything. Tasks added on Home's Today list are picked for today. Click a block on the calendar to edit it in the Day plan.",
            "Start each morning on Home: check the calendar, pick the few tasks that matter, and press Start my day."),
        new Topic("Day plan and calendar",
            "Ryan's design, built on time blocking (planning every part of the day ahead).",
            "The Day plan shows your day as a timeline from 6 AM to 10 PM. Focus sessions are red, breaks green, meetings blue. Click a block and a small editor opens beside it with the title ready to type over; Tab moves through the fields, and Ctrl+Enter or Esc closes it (changes save as you type). Right-click a block to copy, paste, duplicate or delete; drag sessions and breaks to move them; press Delete to remove one. The Calendar tab shows the same plan by day or week, and you can edit there too. In week view, untick Show my plan to see only your calendar events. Use the arrows at the top to plan other days. Changes save automatically.",
            "Plan tomorrow at the end of today, then tick Start this day automatically so the day starts itself. Saved layouts (like a Tuesday layout) are under the Layouts button."),
        new Topic("Fixed times and automatic reshaping",
            "Ryan's design.",
            "Meetings, Lunch, the morning Break, Devotions and the break after each meeting are pinned to their times. When you lengthen a session, the next one gives way; shorten it and the next one grows. If anything ends up outside the allowed lengths (25 to 40 minute sessions, 4 to 7 minute breaks), the stretch around your change is evened out. The day always stays full to its end time.",
            "If one block comes out a minute or two over the limits, it's because the time between fixed blocks can't be split perfectly. Nudge it with the arrows if you like."),
        new Topic("Auto-fill around meetings",
            "Ryan's design, in the spirit of calendar-aware planners like Reclaim and Motion.",
            "Each day is filled from your working-day start to end (6:45 to 4:30 unless you change it in Settings) around the meetings in your calendar, with Devotions at 8:00, Break at 10:05 and Lunch at 12:20. A meeting during the morning break replaces it. Untouched days update themselves when your calendar changes; once you edit a day, it's yours, and Auto-fill day replans it on request.",
            "Set default block lists in Settings, Automatic plans, so every automatic focus session blocks the right apps without extra clicks."),
        new Topic("Adjust working day",
            "Ryan's design.",
            "Change when a single day starts and ends. Sessions and breaks reshape to fill the new hours; fixed blocks outside them drop off. While a day runs, only the end can change.",
            "Use it for a late start or an early finish instead of deleting sessions one by one."),
        new Topic("Focus sessions and Pomodoro",
            "The Pomodoro Technique by Francesco Cirillo, and the focus sessions in Windows 11.",
            "Work in focused sessions separated by short breaks. Day plans run on the clock: start late and you join whichever session is happening now. The Pomodoro and Quick focus tabs run simple sessions without a plan.",
            "Keep sessions single-purpose: one task (or a few quick ones) per session works best."),
        new Topic("Blocking apps, locked and extreme modes",
            "Raycast's Focus mode; locked and extreme modes are Ryan's design.",
            "Blocked apps are closed the moment they open during a focus session, even in the background. Locked mode removes Stop and Skip during focus sessions. Extreme mode also stops you quitting Focus, reopens it if it's force-closed, and survives restarts. The current session can never be shortened by editing.",
            "Use block lists for groups like chat or social apps, and add them to sessions instead of picking apps each time."),
        new Topic("Short breaks from blocking",
            "Ryan's design.",
            "The 3-minute and 8-minute buttons unblock your apps briefly during a focus session. In locked and extreme modes they need two confirmations and typing \"I want to waste time\".",
            "If you reach for these often, the session may be too long or the task too vague: try splitting it into steps."),
        new Topic("Dashboard and ring timer",
            "The focus timer in the Windows 11 Clock app, and Ryan's layout.",
            "The dashboard opens when you start your day. The ring shrinks as the session runs down, with your controls, focus totals and tasks beside and below it. The task card switches between this Session's tasks (the default), Today's tasks and All tasks; in Today and All, + Session adds a task to the session you're in. The calendar on the left shows a moving line for now; drag the divider to resize the two sides.",
            "Keep the dashboard open on a second screen, or minimize Focus and use the small floating timer."),
        new Topic("Notifications",
            "Notification centers in macOS and Windows, kept to what needs a decision.",
            "The bell in the sidebar collects what needs your attention, each with buttons to act on it: a new or cancelled meeting (fit it into your plan, or keep your plan), tasks that appear twice (remove the extra copies; Ctrl+Z undoes it), overdue tasks, reminders, and the end-of-day review. The red number shows how many are waiting.",
            "Clear all empties the list; anything still true is reported again later."),
        new Topic("Floating timer tasks",
            "Ryan's idea.",
            "Click the small caret on the right of the floating timer to drop down your tasks, and tick them off right there. Switch between this session's tasks and today's at the top. The pin keeps the list open until you unpin it or your day ends; unpinned, it folds up when the cursor leaves. Six tasks show before the list scrolls (change it in Settings); drag the grip at the bottom of the list to make it taller for now. The timer shows whenever Focus is open (Ready when nothing runs); its ✕ hides it, and the small 🌡 on the dashboard brings it back. In Settings you can show it only when Focus is minimized.",
            "Pin it on a second screen and work straight down the list."),
        new Topic("Pomodoro with your task list",
            "Ryan's idea.",
            "The Pomodoro tab shows your task list beside the timer, with all its views (this session, today, all, undated). Once a Pomodoro starts, the setup gives way to the countdown on the left, with your tasks on the right. Drag the line between them to resize; Focus remembers it. Untick Show my task list to hide it.",
            "Start a day with just a Pomodoro and your list: pick today's tasks on the right, then press Start."),
        new Topic("Personal tasks",
            "Todoist's separate projects, with a list that stays out of your work.",
            "Personal has its own button in the sidebar. It opens the full Tasks page (every view, search, board, details, export and undo) on a list of its own: its own projects and finished list, completely apart from your work tasks, stats and records, in its own colors. Type tasks the same way (#Project, @label, !, friday, every monday). Press Ctrl+Win+P anywhere in Windows to add one without switching apps. Its look is your choice (Settings, Appearance, Personal tasks look). In Pomodoro, switch the list to Personal and add a task to the running session; finishing it there ticks it off in Personal. The floating timer's list has a Personal choice too. On the phone, swipe sideways between Tasks and Personal.",
            "Keep errands and family things here, so your work list stays about work."),
        new Topic("Settings",
            "Windows 11 and macOS System Settings.",
            "Settings has a category list on the left (General, Appearance, Planning, Focus sessions, Timer and alerts, Tasks, Calendar, Greek practice, Sync and data, Features, Help) and one page at a time on the right. Changes save themselves as you make them; if something can't be saved (like a half-typed time), a note in red under the categories says why. Type in the search box to find any setting on any page (Enter opens its page). Reset this page puts just that page's settings back to how they started. Under Appearance, How tasks look chooses Default, Compact or Paper for your task lists and printouts.",
            "Search is the quickest way in: type lunch, theme or Greek."),
        new Topic("Concepts practice",
            "Spaced repetition, with your own textbook questions.",
            "Enter questions and answers from your reading (Settings, Greek practice, Manage concepts; or paste a whole list, one question = answer per line). They come up like the Greek words: match each question to its answer. Every question stays in review; ones you miss come back sooner and more often. Q&A on the dashboard starts a review any time.",
            "Add the questions right after each reading, while they're fresh."),
        new Topic("When practice comes up, weekends, and extra answers",
            "One schedule: the timelines.",
            "Each practice (words, alphabet, concepts), the book lesson, the evening card review and All-books practice comes up at the times on its own row of Practice timelines, whether or not a focus session is running. Focus only needs to be open (the tray is enough). Anything that lands in a meeting, a break or lunch waits until it's over. Pause all practice on weekends (Greek practice settings, Training settings or the timelines) keeps all of it from popping up on Saturdays and Sundays; you can still start anything yourself. If you're away, missed rounds don't pile up: one round of each comes up when you're back. At the daily reset (4:30 PM unless you change it), times before it stop for the day and anything left on screen while you're away closes; times after the reset still come up and last until midnight. Each practice can also mix in up to three extra answers that don't match anything, to make rounds harder.",
            "Start with one extra answer; add more once rounds feel easy."),
        new Topic("Practice timelines",
            "Your idea: set each round's time yourself.",
            "Settings, Greek practice, Practice timelines shows a row for Words, Alphabet and Concepts on one time line, with today's meetings and lunch shaded. Drag a round to move it (it snaps to 15 minutes), double-click a row to add one, right-click a round to remove it; Add puts one in the biggest gap and Even spreads them out again. The All practice row shows every round together. Use for today changes only today; Save as default makes the times every day's. A round that lands in a meeting, break or lunch waits until it's over.",
            "Put practice right after meetings, when you'd be switching tasks anyway."),
        new Topic("Freedom",
            "Freedom (freedom.to), which you already use.",
            "Settings, Focus sessions, Open Freedom (or Freedom… in the tray menu). Connect your Freedom account once; your password goes only to Freedom and isn't saved. Focus then shows your Freedom blocklists and devices exactly as Freedom has them, syncing when the window opens, every 30 minutes and before each session, so lists you add or delete in Freedom appear or disappear here too. Tick the ones to use and turn on Start Freedom with every focus session: Freedom then runs for the length of each focus session. You can also start a session for any number of minutes, sync, or open the Freedom dashboard.",
            "If Freedom ever stops connecting, use Test connection and send log.txt; Focus's own blocking keeps working meanwhile."),
        new Topic("Alphabet practice",
            "Spaced repetition, with modern Greek pronunciation.",
            "Match letters and letter pairs (like ου, μπ, αυ) to how they sound in modern Greek; letters that change sound show when (for example γ before e and i sounds). No letter names, just sounds. Every letter stays in review: ones you know come back less often, ones you miss come back within 20 minutes, and letters that sound alike (η, ι, υ) are never in the same round. The αβγ button on the dashboard starts a review any time; Settings, Greek practice sets the rounds a day, or switches it off once you know them.",
            "Say each sound out loud as you match it."),
        new Topic("Floating timer and alerts",
            "Raycast's floating focus timer; the full-screen alerts are Ryan's idea.",
            "When Focus is minimized during a session, a small translucent timer floats over your other windows. When a session or break begins on its own, a message flashes across every screen, with a chime.",
            "Drag the floating timer somewhere out of the way; it remembers the spot."),
        new Topic("Block lists",
            "App blockers like Freedom and Cold Turkey.",
            "Name a group of apps once (like Chat or Social) and add the whole list to any session. Changes to a list apply everywhere it's used.",
            "While a session runs you can add apps to a list but not remove them, so you can't unblock something mid-session."),
        new Topic("Tasks on sessions and your task list",
            "Ryan's design.",
            "Your task list sits on the right of the Day plan. Drag a task onto any focus session (in the Day plan or the Calendar's day view) and it shows on the dashboard during that session. The task stays in your list until you finish it, so you can put a big task into several sessions; the list shows how many sessions it's in that day. Switch the list between All and the day you're planning (Today, Tomorrow, or a date) to see just the tasks picked for that day, due that day, or already in its sessions. Finishing it anywhere removes it from the list and from every session. Tasks typed straight into a session work as before: unfinished ones go to the list when the session ends.",
            "Plan the morning by dragging your top three tasks into the first sessions; leave later sessions open for what comes up."),
        new Topic("Priorities, due dates, tags and filters",
            "Todoist (priorities, labels and filters).",
            "Click any task to give it a priority, due date, estimate or tags. All are optional. The filter buttons above the bin show just Today, Overdue, High priority, tasks without a date, or one tag. Most important first sorts overdue, then due today, then today's picks and high priority.",
            "Use only a few tags (like a client name, Admin, Calls). Many tags make filters less useful."),
        new Topic("Today's tasks and Fill today's sessions",
            "Ryan's design, with daily planning in the style of Sunsama.",
            "Click ☀ on the tasks you want to do today (due and overdue tasks count automatically). The line at the top compares the work picked with the focus sessions left today. Fill today's sessions places them into your sessions, most important first.",
            "If the line turns orange, you've picked more than fits. Move some to another day rather than hoping to squeeze them in."),
        new Topic("Estimates and multi-session tasks",
            "Estimating work in pomodoros, from the Pomodoro Technique.",
            "Estimate a task as quick, or 1 to 4 sessions. Quick tasks share a session. A task estimated at several sessions that isn't finished carries on into your next focus session instead of going to the bin. The Resolved list shows how many sessions each task took.",
            "Compare estimates with what tasks actually took; it makes planning more accurate over time."),
        new Topic("Quick add",
            "Todoist's Quick Add.",
            "Press Ctrl+Shift+Space anywhere in Windows (or Ctrl+Alt+Space if another app uses that key) to add a task to the bin without leaving what you're doing.",
            "Capture the thought and get back to work; sort it out later in the bin."),
        new Topic("Everyday dates, times and reminders",
            "Todoist's natural-language entry.",
            "When adding a task you can type things like \"Call Sam tomorrow at 3pm\", \"Report by oct 14\", \"Pay rent every month on the 1st\" or \"in 2 weeks\". The date, time and repeat are taken out of the text. A task with a time gets a reminder from the tray at that time.",
            "Shortcuts work too, like Todoist: @label adds a tag, #Project (or its shortcut) picks a project, /Section a section, ! high priority, !low, due:fri."),
        new Topic("Repeating tasks",
            "Todoist's recurring tasks.",
            "Give a task a repeat like every friday, weekdays, every 2 weeks on monday, every month on the 15th, every last friday or yearly. When you finish it, the next one appears in the bin with its new due date (and reminder).",
            "Use repeats for routines like weekly reports and monthly bills, so they never depend on remembering."),
        new Topic("Steps and notes",
            "Todoist's subtasks and comments.",
            "Break a task into steps you can tick off, and keep notes or links with it. The task shows how many steps are done.",
            "If a task feels too big to start, write its first three steps."),
        new Topic("Upcoming and snooze",
            "Todoist's Upcoming view; snoozing comes from email apps.",
            "Upcoming lists tasks by day: overdue, today, the next two weeks, and later. Snooze hides a task from the bin until a day you choose; it shows in Upcoming meanwhile.",
            "Snooze anything you can't act on yet, so the bin only shows what you can do now."),
        new Topic("Search and undo",
            "Common to task managers like Todoist.",
            "Search filters every task view by text, tags and notes. Undo (or Ctrl+Z when you're not typing) reverses recent changes to tasks and to the Day plan.",
            "Undo keeps your last 30 changes while Focus is open."),
        new Topic("Board",
            "Monday.com boards and Kanban; the Waiting column comes from David Allen's Getting Things Done.",
            "The Board shows your task list in columns: To do, Doing, Waiting and Done today. Drag a card between columns; dropping on Done resolves it. Waiting tasks can say who you're waiting on.",
            "Keep Doing short (one to three tasks). Check Waiting once a day and follow up."),
        new Topic("End-of-day review",
            "Sunsama's daily shutdown, and the reviews in Getting Things Done.",
            "When your day plan ends (or from Review today in the Tasks tab), Focus lists what's unfinished and asks where each goes: tomorrow, another day, back to the bin, or drop it.",
            "Two minutes of review means tomorrow starts with a clear plan instead of yesterday's leftovers."),
        new Topic("Resolved list and counts",
            "Todoist's completed tasks and productivity stats.",
            "Every finished task is kept in Resolved, grouped by day, with counts for today, this week and all time. ↺ sends one back to the bin.",
            "Look at the week's list on Friday; it's a good record for status updates."),
        new Topic("Calendar connection",
            "Outlook and Google's calendar publishing.",
            "Focus reads your calendar through a private link (one way only), refreshes every 15 minutes, and keeps a copy for offline use. Meetings added after you start your day are fitted in automatically.",
            "If a calendar stops updating, publish it again and replace the link in Settings."),
        new Topic("Sync and your phone",
            "Ryan's design, using OneDrive.",
            "Plans, layouts, block lists, your task list and resolved tasks sync through a OneDrive folder. The phone page reads and writes the same task files.",
            "Turn on sync on one computer first, wait for the Focus folder to appear on the other, then turn it on there."),
        new Topic("Command bar",
            "Raycast's command bar.",
            "Press Ctrl+K while Focus is open and type: a tab (\"calendar\"), an action (\"start pomodoro\", \"review today\", \"undo\"), a block list (\"chat\") to start quick focus blocking it, or any task to find it. Start with + to add a task. Use the arrow keys and Enter.",
            "Most things in Focus are three keystrokes away: Ctrl+K, a word, Enter."),
        new Topic("Start-focus key",
            "Raycast's focus shortcut.",
            "Ctrl+Shift+F anywhere in Windows starts a quick focus session with your Quick focus settings and block lists, and stops it again (unless it's locked).",
            "Set Quick focus up once with your usual length and block list, then just press the key when you sit down to work."),
        new Topic("Projects",
            "Todoist's projects.",
            "Create projects in the Tasks sidebar; each gets its own color. Choose a task's project in its details. The Show list gets one filter per project.",
            "Use projects for areas of work (clients, Admin, Personal) and tags for things that cut across them (Calls, Quick)."),
        new Topic("Task templates",
            "Todoist's templates.",
            "Save a set of tasks (one per line, shortcuts allowed) as a template in the Tasks sidebar, and add them all with one click.",
            "Good for repeat processes like onboarding a client or month-end."),
        new Topic("Daily goals and streaks",
            "Todoist's Karma, daily goals and streaks.",
            "Set a goal of tasks finished or hours of focus with blocking (Settings). Reaching either one counts for the day. The sidebar shows today's progress and your streak; weekends never break it.",
            "Pick a goal you can hit on an ordinary day. A streak you keep beats an ambitious one you break."),
        new Topic("Points, levels and badges",
            "Todoist's Karma: points, levels and streaks.",
            "Stats opens with your level: points come from finishing tasks (10 each), focus with blocking (1 per 5 minutes) and meeting your daily goal (25). Levels go Starter, Steady, Focused, Pro, Expert, Master, Grandmaster, Zen. Below are your streaks, highlights and records, twelve badges to earn, and patterns like your most productive weekday and hour. It's all worked out from your history, so both computers always agree. With Show weekends off (Settings), weekends stay out of the charts and the streak, but weekend work still counts toward totals, points and badges.",
            "Use the next level and the nearest badge as small targets for the week. Switch it off in Features if it's not your thing."),
        new Topic("Stats and workload",
            "Monday's dashboards and workload view.",
            "Stats shows focus hours and tasks finished for the last four weeks, how your estimates compare with how long tasks really took, and the next seven days' planned work against your focus sessions (red means overbooked).",
            "Check the workload on Monday morning and move tasks off red days before the week starts."),
        new Topic("Automations",
            "Monday's automations.",
            "Three simple rules, each with its own switch in Features: overdue tasks become high priority, tasks left in Doing go back to To do each morning, and tasks Waiting for several days (you choose how many) get a reminder.",
            "Leave on the ones that save you thinking; switch off any that get in your way."),
        new Topic("Age filters",
            "Ryan's idea.",
            "The Show list has \"+ one week old\" and \"+ ten days old\" to find tasks that have been sitting around. Tasks from before this was tracked count from the day you installed it.",
            "Once a week, open \"+ ten days old\" and decide: schedule it, snooze it, or delete it."),
        new Topic("Typing shortcuts (like Todoist)",
            "Todoist's Quick Add.",
            "When adding a task: @label adds a tag (@email), #Project puts it in a project (#Client A; a new name creates the project), /Section puts it in an existing section of that project (#Client A /Legal), ! makes it high priority and !low low. Give a project short shortcuts under Projects in the Tasks sidebar, so #goj or #1 means Gospel of John.",
            "Keep project shortcuts to two or three letters you'd never type by accident."),
        new Topic("Undated tasks",
            "Ryan's idea.",
            "Undated shows tasks with no due date that aren't picked for any day, in Tasks, the Day plan, the dashboard and the phone. They're the ones most likely to be forgotten.",
            "Once a week, give each undated task a day, snooze it, or delete it."),
        new Topic("Tasks on focus blocks and tasks with a time",
            "Ryan's idea, with time blocking from Cal Newport.",
            "Each focus session on the calendar can show its tasks as a small red numbered list (switch it off in Features). A task with a time (\"Call Sam at 2pm\") goes into the focus session at that time, or the next one if that's a break or meeting, and follows along if the plan changes.",
            "Give your one or two most important tasks a time, and the rest a day."),
        new Topic("Extending a session",
            "Ryan's idea.",
            "Ctrl+E or the Extend button opens a slider starting at 10 minutes; slide it and press Enter. In a day plan, the extra time comes from the blocks that follow, while meetings, lunch and the end of your day stay put.",
            "Extend when you're in flow; take the break when you're stuck."),
        new Topic("New meetings, filling part of the day, and leftover time",
            "Calendar-aware planners like Reclaim and Motion.",
            "When a meeting appears after you've planned a day, Focus asks whether to fit it into your plan or show it on top (Settings changes this). To refill only part of a day, drag across the calendar (or use Fill part of the day) and Focus replaces the sessions and breaks in that range, keeping meetings and fixed breaks. Auto-fill avoids awkward gaps by stretching nearby blocks; any short Free time left can be clicked to make it a focus session.",
            "After a morning of interruptions, refill just the afternoon instead of replanning the whole day."),
        new Topic("The floating timer's tasks",
            "Raycast's floating timer.",
            "Hover over the small floating timer to see this session's tasks (or the next session's during a break) and tick them off. It shrinks back as soon as your cursor leaves.",
            "Keep the timer near where you work, so checking off a task takes one move."),
        new Topic("Export and print",
            "Todoist's print and export.",
            "The printer icon beside the Tasks title and the printer button on the dashboard offer: this session, today's plan (a one-page day sheet), today, tomorrow, this week, upcoming, overdue, undated, waiting, all tasks, each project or tag, and what's done today or this week. Click a list to print it; choose Microsoft Print to PDF in the print window to save a PDF.",
            "Print today's plan each morning and keep it beside your keyboard."),
        new Topic("Daily records and Obsidian",
            "Obsidian's daily notes.",
            "Every day gets a Markdown record: focus time, sessions, tasks finished and added, the plan, your meetings, goal and streak, with properties for Obsidian's Bases and Dataview. A My notes section at the bottom is never overwritten. Choose an Obsidian folder and records go only there; if the vault is unavailable they go to your Focus folder and move over when it's back.",
            "Write one line in My notes at the end of each day; in a year it's a remarkable record."),
        new Topic("Greek practice",
            "Spaced-repetition flashcards (the Leitner system) and Duolingo-style matching. Meanings are Jeff Dodson's public-domain Greek-English lexicon, word for word; word counts from the SBL Greek New Testament via MorphGNT (CC BY-SA).",
            "A few times a day (Settings, Greek practice) a round of 3 to 10 words pops up on the screen you're working on (Esc puts it off for 20 minutes), and the Αα button on the dashboard starts a review any time, for as many rounds as you like (pick the round size as you go): match each Greek word to its meaning and press Go; any you miss come back until they're right. Rounds never appear during meetings or lunch: they wait for the next focus session or break. Each word climbs six levels as you get it right on the first try, waiting longer each time (2 hours, then 1, 3, 7, 14 and 30 days) and every word in My words keeps coming back (never more than 30 days apart), with words you miss more often coming back sooner and showing up more in reviews. No word retires until My words holds more than your threshold (100 by default, in Settings); after that, only the most familiar words retire, just enough to keep My words at that size, and they're checked about every 90 days. The slider sets how well a word must be known (3 to 12 right rounds) before it can retire. A miss drops it back two levels and brings it back within the hour. Manage words searches every word of the Greek New Testament (5,399, most frequent first, with how often each occurs) by Greek (accents optional) or English; add them as they are, edit them first, or add your own.",
            "Keep it to four words a round and four rounds a day: little and often beats long sessions. The word list's meanings are short glosses; edit any to match your course."),
        new Topic("Keyboard shortcuts",
            "Standard Windows shortcuts, Todoist and Raycast.",
            "Press F1 (or Ctrl+/) for the full list. The essentials: Ctrl+N new task, Ctrl+F search, Ctrl+K command bar, Ctrl+1 to 8 switch tabs, Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y undo and redo, Ctrl+P print, Ctrl+Left/Right change day, Ctrl+T today, Space pause, Ctrl+E extend, Esc close, Ctrl+Enter save and close.",
            "Learn three this week: Ctrl+N, Ctrl+K and Ctrl+Z."),
        new Topic("Themes",
            "Ryan's design.",
            "Classic (the warm red), Monochrome (black, white and grays, crisp like print) and Calm (sage, dusty blue and teal), each in light and dark. Pick one in Settings, Appearance; the phone app has the same three under its menu.",
            "Try Calm for long planning sessions and Monochrome when you print."),
        new Topic("Themes and customization",
            "Ryan's design.",
            "A clean, Todoist-inspired look with a sidebar, in light and dark. Shrink the sidebar to just its icons with the arrow at its top or Ctrl+\\ (names show when you hover). Display size, task text size, calendar spacing, alert and warning lengths, and your working hours are all in Settings. Features from the latest updates can be switched off individually.",
            "If the window feels crowded on a laptop, try Display size Smaller and Calendar spacing Compact.")
    };
}
